00:43:45.580 --> 00:44:31.240
<v Shalom Auslander>Where in New York are you from, asked Martin. Upstate, she whispered, as she carried our exhausted son into the room. I spent a lot of time there, said Marvin. Hudson Valley, I think it was. Might have been the Catskills. Yeah, I think it was the Catskills. My wife and I used to take our kids up there when they were younger. OK, I whispered. We don't talk much anymore. My kids, I mean, not my wife. She's dead. Sure, I whispered. Well, good night. I closed the door behind me and locked it. Jesus Christ, I said. You OK, asked my wife. I'm fine, I said, with a smile.
<v Shalom Auslander>“你们是从纽约哪里来的，”马丁问。“上州，”她低声说，把精疲力竭的儿子抱进房间。“我在那儿待过很长时间，”马文说。“哈德逊河谷，我想是。可能是卡茨基尔山。是的，我想是卡茨基尔山。我妻子和我以前在孩子小的时候经常带他们去那儿。”“好吧，”我低声说。“我们不再多说话了。我的孩子们，我是说，不是我妻子。她去世了。”“当然，”我低声说。“好吧，晚安。”我锁上身后的门。天哪，我说。“你还好吗，”我妻子问。“我很好，”我说，带着微笑。

00:44:31.240 --> 00:45:15.740
<v Shalom Auslander>We put our son to bed, crept quietly out the back door, and lay down together on a lounge chair underneath the stars. I love you, said my wife. I love you too, I answered. [CLEARING THROAT] came the sound from next door. [COUGHING] We lay there a while longer, holding hands in the cool, tropical night breeze, watching the shimmering lights of long dead stars, and pretending we weren't listening to an old man drowning in his own phlegm. [COUGHING]
