00:45:59.290 --> 00:46:38.730
<v Shalom Auslander>Good morning, said Marvin. He was sitting at his patio table, finishing his breakfast. Good morning, I said. A lovely day, isn't it? Oh, it is, he said. Mornings like this, my wife and I used to get up early and go for a long swim. That sounds nice, I said. She's dead now, he said. Hey, I said, who isn't? That's true, said Marvin. I looked out over the beach and smiled. Check and mate, Marv. I was unflappable.
<v Shalom Auslander>“早安，”马文说。他正坐在露台桌旁吃早餐。“早安，”我说。“天气不错，不是吗？”“哦，是啊，”他说。“这样的早晨，我和我妻子过去常常早起去游泳。”“听起来不错，”我说。“她现在去世了，”他说。“嘿，”我说，“谁不是呢？”“那倒是，”马文说。我望向海滩，微笑了。将军，马文。我是临危不乱的。

00:46:38.730 --> 00:47:23.760
<v Shalom Auslander>Auschwitz, said Marvin. I turned around. Sorry? My wife, she died in Auschwitz. We were quite young, only married a few years. Auschwitz, I said. Marvin nodded. We were sent to Dachau at first. But after a few weeks, they sent us to Auschwitz. Auschwitz, I said. Sure, my mother too. She was sent to Bergen-Belsen, but that's not where she died. She died in Auschwitz with my father. Probably had pneumonia. That's what I heard. My father was shot. The Nazis shot him. I know this from some people in Miami who knew him in the camps. Ethel and Morris Goldstein. They died a while ago.
<v Shalom Auslander>“奥斯威辛，”马文说。我转过身。“对不起？”“我的妻子，她死在奥斯威辛。我们很年轻，结婚才几年。”“奥斯威辛，”我说。马文点点头。“我们起初被送到达豪。但在几周后，他们把我们送到了奥斯威辛。”“奥斯威辛，”我说。“当然，我妈妈也是。她被送到卑尔根-贝尔森，但她不是死在那里。她和我爸爸死在奥斯威辛。可能得了肺炎。那是我的耳闻。我爸爸被枪杀了。纳粹枪杀了他。我从迈阿密的一些在集中营认识他的人那里知道这件事。埃塞尔和莫里斯·戈德斯坦。他们很久以前就去世了。”

00:47:23.760 --> 00:48:05.340
<v Shalom Auslander>I should have been compassionate, I know. I should have taken a pad and pen and committed his story to paper for future generations. But I didn't. Instead, I seethed. 20 minutes of genocide stories later, I went into our villa, closed the doors sharply behind me, and stood in the center of the room with my hands on my hips. What, asked my wife. I threw my hand into the air. Auschwitz, I said. Pardon? Auschwitz. What are you talking about, she asked. Au-freaking-schwitz. He's a survivor, hon. A Holocaust survivor.
<v Shalom Auslander>我本该有同情心，我知道。我本该拿纸笔把他的故事写下来留给后代。但我没有。相反，我怒不可遏。20分钟的种族灭绝故事之后，我走进别墅，重重地关上门，双手叉腰站在房间中央。“怎么了，”我妻子问。我把手往空中一扔。“奥斯威辛，”我说。“什么？”“奥斯威辛。”“你在说什么，”她问。“奥——该死的——斯威辛。他是幸存者，亲爱的。一个大屠杀幸存者。”

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:33.970
<v Shalom Auslander>I don't have anything against Holocaust survivors. Some of my best friends are Holocaust survivors. OK, that's not actually true. But I don't have anything against them. But if I want to relax and forget about life for a while, maybe hit a bar and have a drink, I'm not going to call Elie Wiesel. Hey, Elie, how's it going? I had a tough day. Why don't you come over and we'll watch Schindler's List? Bring beer.
<v Shalom Auslander>我不反对大屠杀幸存者。我最好的朋友里有些是大屠杀幸存者。好吧，那其实不是真的。但我不反对他们。但如果我想放松一下，暂时忘记生活，也许去酒吧喝一杯，我不会给埃利·威塞尔打电话。“嘿，埃利，怎么样？我今天过得很糟。为什么你不过来，我们看《辛德勒的名单》？带上啤酒。”

00:48:33.970 --> 00:48:57.350
<v Shalom Auslander>A Holocaust survivor, I said, pacing back and forth across the room. The place is half empty, and the guy next door is a Holocaust survivor. I think I've been pretty good about this. I didn't let the travel upset me. I didn't let the hacking next door all night long get to me. But this is too much. It's too much. I'm standing in paradise talking about gas chambers.
<v Shalom Auslander>“一个大屠杀幸存者，”我说，在房间里来回踱步。“这里一半是空的，隔壁那家伙是个大屠杀幸存者。我觉得我已经表现得很好了。我没有让行程扰乱我。我没有让隔壁整夜的咳嗽声影响我。但这太过分了。太过分了。我站在天堂里讨论毒气室。”

00:48:57.350 --> 00:49:19.690
<v Shalom Auslander>My wife was sympathetic, but she'd seen this before and insisted I was blowing it out of proportion. As usual, she said. And I said, what's that supposed to mean? And she said, you know what that means. And I said, no, I don't. And less than 24 hours after our plane touched down in Eden, we were fighting.
<v Shalom Auslander>我妻子很有同情心，但她以前见过这种场面，并坚持认为我是在小题大做。“一如既往，”她说。我问，“那是什么意思？”她说，“你知道那是什么意思。”我说，“不，我不知道。”在我们到达伊甸园不到24小时后，我们就吵架了。

00:49:19.690 --> 00:49:47.110
<v Shalom Auslander>It's your decision, she said to me, as she gathered her beach things together. He's not ruining our vacation, you are. No I'm not, I shouted. Goebbels is. Blame Goebbels. She walked out and stomped down the patio steps. My son began to cry. A dozen iguanas sat on the deck. Two of them ran inside and hid under the bed. Damn it, I thought.
<v Shalom Auslander>“这是你的决定，”她一边收拾沙滩用品一边对我说。“他没有毁掉我们的假期，是你。不，我没有，”我大喊。“戈培尔才是。去怪戈培尔。”她走出去，跺着脚走下露台台阶。我儿子开始哭。十几只鬣蜥坐在甲板上。其中两只跑进屋里躲在床下。该死，我心想。

00:49:47.110 --> 00:50:05.040
<v Shalom Auslander>I spent the next few hours avoiding my wife, hoping that a little time would settle things down between us. I took my son and walked over to the front office. Did you feed them, the man asked. We told you not to feed them. They're in our room, I said. They're under our bed.
<v Shalom Auslander>接下来的几个小时我都在避开我妻子，希望时间能平息我们之间的矛盾。我带着儿子走到前台。“你喂它们了吗，”那人问。“我们告诉过你不要喂它们。它们在我们的房间里，”我说。“它们躲在我们的床底下。”

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:22.600
<v Shalom Auslander>I heard laughter coming from the lobby and poked my head around the corner. Marvin was sitting in a wicker chair, surrounded by a half a dozen adoring hotel employees. A waiter from the bar brought him a rum punch. Let me pay you, said Marvin. No, no, Mr. Marvin, said the waiter. No, no.
<v Shalom Auslander>我听到从大堂传来笑声，探头一看。马文正坐在柳条椅上，被半打崇拜他的酒店员工包围。酒吧的一位服务员给他端来一杯朗姆潘趣酒。“让我付钱，”马文说。“不，不，马文先生，”服务员说。“不，不。”

00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:50.240
<v Shalom Auslander>If there's anything worse than hating someone, it's discovering that everyone else loves them. Oh, Mr. Marvin, an attractive female chambermaid cried, he's terrible. The man at the front desk smiled in Marvin's direction. What a sweet man, he said, and then shook his head. And what he's been through.
<v Shalom Auslander>如果有什么比恨一个人更糟糕，那就是发现其他人都在爱他。“哦，马文先生，”一位迷人的女客房服务员喊道，他太可怜了。前台的男人朝着马文的方向微笑着。多么甜蜜的人，他说，然后摇了摇头。而且他经历了那么多。

00:50:50.240 --> 00:51:24.470
<v Shalom Auslander>At dinner that evening, Marvin sat at the resort owner's table. My wife and I sat nearby, barely talking. Marvin told funny stories, and everyone laughed. Marvin told sad stories-- about the Holocaust I guessed, judging by the horror on the face of his dinner companions-- and everyone hugged him. And when the bill came and Marvin reached for it, again, they refused to let him pay. That was when I decided, with absolute certainty, that the son of a bitch was faking it.
<v Shalom Auslander>那天晚上吃晚餐时，马文坐在度假村主人的桌旁。我和妻子坐在附近，几乎没说话。马文讲有趣的故事，大家都在笑。马文讲悲伤的故事——我猜是关于大屠杀的，看着他同桌用餐者脸上的恐惧——大家都拥抱他。当账单来了，马文伸手去拿时，他们再次拒绝让他付钱。就在那时，我断定，那个狗娘养的是装的。

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:50.340
<v Shalom Auslander>Holocaust survivor my ass, I thought. I grew up in an Orthodox Jewish community. I've known Holocaust survivors, OK? And they don't go on and on about it. Elie Wiesel doesn't go on about it. Marvin was no Holocaust survivor. He was just an old chatterbox looking for a meal ticket. There's no such thing as a free lunch until you tell everyone sitting next to you at lunch about your stay in Bergen-Belsen.
<v Shalom Auslander>“什么大屠杀幸存者，去他的，”我想。我在一个正统犹太社区长大。我知道大屠杀幸存者，好吗？他们不会没完没了地谈论它。埃利·威塞尔就不会没完没了地谈论它。马文根本不是什么大屠杀幸存者。他只是一个找饭票的老碎嘴。在你告诉午餐桌旁的所有人关于你在卑尔根-贝尔森的经历之前，世上没有免费的午餐。

00:51:50.340 --> 00:52:02.000
<v Shalom Auslander>Marvin was faking, I was sure of it. And I was equally sure that if I could just prove that to my wife, all would be forgiven. And I knew just how to do it.
<v Shalom Auslander>马文在装，我确信。而且我也同样确信，只要我能向我妻子证明这一点，一切都会得到原谅。我知道怎么做。

00:52:02.000 --> 00:52:21.540
<v Shalom Auslander>We came back from dinner and I put my son into his pajamas and read him some stories. My wife sang him some songs and put him to bed. And after that, she put on her shoes and said she needed to take a walk. Fine, I said. Good, she answered. Great, I said. Whatever, she replied.
<v Shalom Auslander>我们吃完晚饭回来，我给儿子穿上睡衣读了些故事。我妻子给他唱了几首歌哄他入睡。之后，她穿上鞋说她需要出去走走。“好吧，”我说。“好，”她回答。“太棒了，”我说。“随便，”她回复。

00:52:21.540 --> 00:52:41.780
<v Shalom Auslander>I went outside, sat down on the patio steps, and watched the ocean, hoping it would calm me down. Suddenly, I heard a groan coming from Marvin's patio. I stood up, peeked over the fence that separated our two patios, and saw him asleep on one of his lounge chairs. This was my chance.
<v Shalom Auslander>我走到外面，坐在露台台阶上，看着大海，希望它能让我平静下来。突然，我听到从马文的露台传来一声呻吟。我站起来，偷看隔开我们两个露台的围栏，看到他睡在其中一张躺椅上。这是我的机会。

00:52:41.780 --> 00:53:09.430
<v Shalom Auslander>I looked around to see if anyone was watching, went over to his patio, and crept up the stairs. He rustled, and I froze. After a few seconds, I tiptoed over to where he was sleeping. His left arm was propped behind his head, but his right arm was stretched out along the arm of the chair. Bingo. I made my way back to our room and waited anxiously for my wife to return.
<v Shalom Auslander>我环顾四周看看有没有人看，走到他的露台，蹑手蹑脚地走上楼梯。他动了动，我僵住了。几秒钟后，我踮着脚尖走到他睡觉的地方。他的左手臂撑在头后，但他的右手臂伸在椅子的扶手上。宾果。我走回我们的房间，焦虑地等待我妻子的归来。

00:53:09.430 --> 00:53:48.450
<v Shalom Auslander>A few minutes later, the front door opened. Well, well, well, I said, as she came into the room. Well, well, what, she asked. Well, well, well, I guess Mr. Auschwitz isn't such a survivor after all. What are you talking about, she asked. I held my arm out and pointed to my forearm. No numbers, I said with a smile. At last, I'd gotten a clean look at his forearm, and no numbers. What, asked my wife. No numbers. If he was in the Holocaust, where are the numbers?
<v Shalom Auslander>几分钟后，前台的门开了。“嗯，嗯，嗯，”当她进屋时，我说。“嗯，嗯，什么，”她问。“嗯，嗯，嗯，我猜奥斯威辛先生毕竟不是什么幸存者。”“你在说什么，”她问。我伸出手臂，指着我的前臂。“没有编号，”我微笑着说。最后，我清楚地看到了他的前臂，没有编号。“什么，”我妻子问。“没有编号。如果他在大屠杀中，编号在哪里？”

00:53:48.450 --> 00:54:09.230
<v Shalom Auslander>Her face dropped. What did you do, she asked. What? What did you do? I didn't do anything. Did you ask him to show you his numbers? Bloody hell, Shal, did you ask him to show you his numbers? I didn't ask him to show me his numbers, I said. He was sleeping.
<v Shalom Auslander>她的脸沉了下来。“你做了什么，”她问。“什么？你做了什么？”“我什么都没做。你让他给你看他的编号了吗？该死，沙尔，你让他给你看编号了吗？”“我没让他给我看编号，”我说。“他在睡觉。”

00:54:09.230 --> 00:54:42.380
<v Shalom Auslander>She pressed her fingertips against her eyes and shook her head. And why exactly would he lie about being in the Holocaust? Why, I asked. Free stuff. They pay for all his meals. They probably pay for his room. He's faking it. This is ludicrous. Why would he pick the Holocaust? What's he going to say, Bataan Death March? It was a hike. Get over it. You want free stuff, you go with Holocaust.
<v Shalom Auslander>她用指尖按住眼睛，摇了摇头。“他到底为什么要在大屠杀这件事上撒谎？为什么，”我问。“免费的东西。他们支付他所有的餐费。他们可能付他的房费。他是装的。这太荒谬了。他为什么选大屠杀？他要说什么，巴丹死亡行军？那是一次远足。算了吧。如果你想要免费的东西，你会选大屠杀。”

00:54:42.380 --> 00:55:23.020
<v Shalom Auslander>Which arm, she asked. What? Which arm did you check? I paused. The right one, I said. She looked at me. They tattooed the left? She nodded. And they didn't tattoo everyone. How do you know? My neighbors were survivors, she said. Both neighbors. None of them had numbers. She kicked her shoes off and headed for the bathroom. This isn't about Marvin, she said. This isn't about numbers or concentration camps. This is about you.
<v Shalom Auslander>“哪只胳膊，”她问。“什么？你检查了哪只胳膊？”我停顿了一下。“右边，”我说。她看着我。“他们纹在左边？”她点点头。“而且他们没有纹每个人。你怎么知道？”“我的邻居是幸存者，”她说。“两个邻居。他们都没有编号。”她踢掉鞋子朝浴室走去。“这与马文无关，”她说。“这与编号或集中营无关。这是关于你。”

00:55:23.020 --> 00:56:16.730
<v Shalom Auslander>All that night and into the next morning, I couldn't sleep. I lay in bed, imagining that I'd prove to everyone what a fraud Marvin was. I pictured finding a photo of him, circa 1940, somewhere in Miami. I pictured calling the Holocaust Museum in DC. Marvin, they would say. We have no record of a Marvin. I pictured confronting him at dinner, catching him out on some esoteric German historical fact. Wrong, Himmler didn't take over the Gestapo until 1934. Ha. But mostly, I just felt awful. A full third of our first family vacation was over, and we'd spent most of it fighting. I was sure our son sensed it, sure that he would hold it against me, sure I was wrong, but sure I was right.
<v Shalom Auslander>那天晚上一直到第二天早晨，我都没能睡着。我躺在床上，想象着我向每个人证明马文是一个骗子。我想象着找到他在1940年左右的照片，在迈阿密的某个地方。我想象着给华盛顿特区的大屠杀纪念馆打电话。“马文，”他们会说。“我们没有马文的记录。”我想象着在晚餐时与他对质，揭穿他关于某个深奥的德国历史事实。“错了，希姆莱直到1934年才接管盖世太保。哈。”但大多时候，我只是感到糟糕透顶。我们的第一次家庭度假已经过去三分之一了，我们大部分时间都在吵架。我确信我儿子感觉到了，确信他会因此记恨我，确信我错了，但确信我当时是对的。

00:56:16.730 --> 00:56:47.860
<v Shalom Auslander>At the first sign of daybreak, I got out of bed and went down to the beach. I walked south along the entire length of the shore before turning around and heading back. As I grew closer to the beach across from our villa, I saw something dark and heavy in the shallow water. I thought it was a log or a mass of seaweed, but then I saw it was moving. It was kicking. It was a person. And as I grew closer still, I realized it was Marvin. It was Marvin, and he was struggling to get to shore.
<v Shalom Auslander>黎明的第一缕曙光，我起床走到海滩。我沿着整个海岸线向南走，然后转身走回来。当我走近我们别墅对面的海滩时，我看到浅水中有一些深色且重的东西。我以为那是圆木或一团海藻，但我看到它在移动。它在踢水。是个人。当我更靠近时，我意识到是马文。是马文，他正挣扎着想上岸。

00:56:47.860 --> 00:57:09.290
<v Shalom Auslander>I hate to admit it, but even as I ran to help him, I was pissed off. It was bad enough I had to put up with this pain in the ass. Now I had to save him? I grabbed Marvin around his chest, lifting and pulling as I swung his arm across my shoulder. He was coughing spluttering, trying to catch his breath. I'm all right, he was saying, I'm all right.
<v Shalom Auslander>我讨厌承认这一点，但即使当我跑去帮他时，我也火大。我已经忍受这个累赘够久了。现在我还要救他？我搂住马文的胸口，把他举起来拖向岸边，把他的胳膊架在我的肩上。他咳嗽着，气喘吁吁。“我没事，”他说，“我没事。”

00:57:09.290 --> 00:58:15.580
<v Shalom Auslander>I helped him onto the beach. I was breathing heavily. That, I said, was the most pathetic cry for attention I have ever seen. Marvin laughed and took a moment to catch his breath. My wife, he said. Yeah, I said. Auschwitz. He shook his head. Second wife, he said. We came here together for 25 years. We used to get up for early morning swims. As I got older, it got harder for me to get back to the shore, undertow and all. She would stand at the edge and wait for me to come back. She sounds great, I said. She was, said Marvin. I shook my head. Such a shame she wasted all those years with you. Marvin laughed again. We have the same sense of humor, he said. We should spend more time together. I stood up, wiped the sand from my legs and hands, and uttered the most honest thing I'd said to Marvin since I met him. That sounds awful.
<v Shalom Auslander>我把他扶上沙滩。我呼吸沉重。“那，”我说，“是我见过最可怜的求关注方式。”马文笑了笑，花点时间喘口气。“我妻子，”他说。“是啊，”我说。“奥斯威辛。”他摇摇头。“第二个妻子，”他说。“我们在这里一起度过了25年。我们过去常早起游泳。随着我年龄增长，我回到岸上变得更困难了，暗流什么的。她会站在边缘等我回来。”“她听起来很棒，”我说。“她确实很棒，”马文说。我摇摇头。“真是可惜她和你浪费了那么多年。”马文又笑了。“我们有相同的幽默感，”他说。“我们应该多在一起待待。”我站起来，抹掉腿和手上的沙子，说出了我见到马文以来对他说的最诚实的话。“那听起来太糟糕了。”

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:33.810
<v Shalom Auslander>Marvin smiled and we walked back to our rooms. And after that moment, he never bothered me again. I'd wasted three days trying to be polite to someone I couldn't stand and nearly ruined another vacation. But by finally being the ass I really am, I'd saved it.
<v Shalom Auslander>马文微笑着，我们走回房间。从那一刻起，他再也没有打扰过我。我浪费了三天试图对一个我无法忍受的人表现礼貌，并差点毁了另一个假期。但通过终于成为那个真实的我，我拯救了它。

00:58:33.810 --> 00:58:52.880
<v Shalom Auslander>I was cordial enough. Marvin waved when I saw him in the TV room, telling Holocaust stories to the young couple who checked in that evening. And I waved back. He nodded when I saw him at breakfast the following morning, telling a pair of young waitresses about mass graves in Sobibor. And I nodded back.
<v Shalom Auslander>我表现得足够有礼貌。我在电视室看到他时向马文挥手，他正给那天晚上入住的年轻夫妇讲大屠杀故事。我也挥手回应。第二天早晨吃早餐时看到他，他正在告诉那两个年轻女服务员关于索比堡的大规模墓地。我也点点头回应。

00:58:52.880 --> 00:59:08.900
<v Shalom Auslander>And then we finished our breakfast. And my wife took my hand in hers, and she smiled at me. And I smiled at her. And together we walked, arm in arm, to watch the sun, still rising, over the unspoiled beach below.
<v Shalom Auslander>然后我们吃完早餐。我妻子牵起我的手，向我微笑。我也对她微笑。我们挽着手一起走，去看太阳在下方的原始海滩上冉冉升起。

00:59:08.900 --> 00:59:17.280
<v Ira Glass>Shalom Auslander is the author, most recently, of the memoir, Foreskin's Lament, which is out in hardback, and comes out in paperback October 4.
<v Ira Glass>沙洛姆·奥斯兰德是回忆录《包皮的挽歌》的作者，该书近期出版，平装本将于10月4日面市。

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:28.990
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Robyn Semien and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Jane Feltes, Lisa Pollak, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Adrianne Mathiowetz runs our website. Production help from Seth Lind and P.J. Vogt. Music help from Jessica Hopper.
